WEBVTT

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:18.640
Do you hear?  Hooves beating

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:30.750
A carriage goes through the gentle hills

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:38.030
Someone comes to pick me up

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:45.980
And takes me

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:49.950
To the avenue with white flowers

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:58.070
To the home of wind

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.450
He takes me

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.900
I'm sure

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:02.420
It was dangerous!

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:16.290
Good afternoon, Miss Cuthbert.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:22.640
What are you doing here? Go away!

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:30.400
You're the last folks I was looking for today.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:33.580
Come inside.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:35.620
And how are you, Anne?

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:38.360
I'm as well as can be expected, thank you.

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:40.280
I see. That's good to hear.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.170
Well... Mrs. Spencer, we're here because...

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:44.450
Stop!

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:47.740
That´s dangerous.

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:51.170
I got You.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:52.410
No!

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:55.550
It´s Anne!

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:00.270
Good afternoon, Miss Cuthbert.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:01.770
Good afternoon, Flora.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:18.550
Lily has been very good.
She's already adapted to life here.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:22.290
They are as close as real sisters.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.000
My husband is very glad for them too.

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:30.980
Lily, you...

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:37.000
The fact is, Mrs. Spencer, there's
been a queer mistake somewhere.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:46.540
We send word, for you to bring us a boy.

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:48.720
What did you say?

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.200
We were thinking of taking in a boy.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:54.860
You don't say so!

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:56.090
Why...

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:03.480
Robert sent word down by his daughter
Nancy and she said you wanted a girl.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:08.650
What should we do now?

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:12.610
But it certainly wasn't my fault,
you see, Miss Cuthbert.

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:16.060
I thought I was following your instructions.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.060
It was our own fault.

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:24.060
We should have come to you ourselves,
since it was such an important message.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:29.460
Anyhow, the mistake has been made and
the only thing to do is to set it right.

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:32.440
Can we send the child back to the asylum?

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:45.850
I suppose they'll take her back, won't they?

00:04:45.850 --> 00:04:48.360
I suppose so.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:52.140
But I don't think it will be
necessary to send her back.

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:56.390
Mrs. Blewett was up here yesterday,

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.160
and she was saying how much she wished
she'd sent by me for a little girl to help her.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:01.540
Mrs. Blewett?

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:06.710
She has a large family, you know,
and she finds it hard to get help.

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:08.990
Anne will be the very girl for her.

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:11.610
Is it that Mrs. Blewett...

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:19.840
You don't get this chance everyday.
It's the lord's blessing!

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:28.070
And if there isn't Mrs. Blewett coming
up the lane this blessed minute!

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:36.980
She came at the right time.
Excuse me for a while.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:38.770
Mrs. Blewett.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:03.800
What a strage coincidence.
It's the lord's blessing.

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:08.030
I'm talking about her.

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:28.200
How old are you and what's your name?

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:30.370
Well?

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:35.060
I asked how old you are.

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:37.300
She's eleven, Mrs. Blewett.

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:40.830
I'm sorry, but I would like
the girl to speak for herself.

00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:49.010
Well, let's not stand outside and talk.

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:51.320
Come on into the house.

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:53.300
Come in, please.

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:56.920
Flora, go in and put the kettle on.

00:06:57.610 --> 00:06:58.870
Yes, mother.

00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:12.040
What do you think, Mrs. Blewett?

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.010
I think she'll be just the thing for you.

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:22.770
Well, you don't look as if there
was much to you. But you're wiry.

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:30.070
I don't know but the wiry
ones are the best after all.

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:33.060
I like her strong character as well.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:41.020
Well, if I take you you'll have to be a good girl.

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:44.170
Good and smart and respectful.

00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:48.200
I'll expect you to earn your keep,
and no mistake about that.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.020
Yes, I suppose I might as well
take her off your hands.

00:07:55.700 --> 00:07:59.670
The baby's awful fractious, and I'm
clean worn out attending to him.

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:01.850
I can rest a little now.

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:05.230
I really didn't know what to do.

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:09.650
If you like I can take her home right now.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:16.730
Well, I don't know.

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:27.000
I didn't say that we had absolutely
decided that we wouldn't keep her.

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:34.870
In fact I may say that
Matthew is disposed to keep her.

00:08:34.870 --> 00:08:37.960
I just came over to find out how the
mistake had occurred.

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:41.830
I think I'd better take her home again
and talk it over with Matthew.

00:08:42.890 --> 00:08:48.580
If we make up our mind not to keep her
we'll bring her over to you tomorrow night.

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:55.640
If we don't, you may know that she
is going to stay with us.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:09.130
Will that suit you, Mrs. Blewett?

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:13.080
I suppose it'll have to,
You were the one who sent for her first.

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:15.940
I came her for a recipe actually.

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:19.660
Really, I talked too much then.

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:21.640
Please, wait a moment.

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:32.200
Miss Cuthbert...

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:17.620
Oh, Miss Cuthbert,
did you really say that perhaps

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:19.620
you would let me stay at Green Gables?

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:21.760
Is that true?

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:25.550
Did you really say it?

00:11:29.140 --> 00:11:31.030
Or did I only imagine that you did?

00:11:32.300 --> 00:11:36.550
I think you'd better learn to control
that imagination of yours

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:40.090
if you can't distinguish between
what is real and what isn't.

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:43.780
Yes, you did hear me say that.

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:47.060
But it isn't decided yet.

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:51.560
And perhaps we will conclude to let
Mrs. Blewett take you after all.

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:55.640
She certainly needs you
much more than I do.

00:11:55.950 --> 00:11:59.540
I'd rather go back to the asylum
than go to live with her.

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:02.770
She looks exactly like a... like a gimlet.

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:03.870
Gimlet?

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:15.460
A little girl like you should be ashamed of
talking so about a lady and a stranger.

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:16.190
I'm going to park the buggy, go on upstairs.

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:19.000
You can tell me if you need any help.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:22.510
There's no need for that.
I'll call you when dinner's ready.

00:13:39.800 --> 00:13:41.090
I'm home.

00:13:41.090 --> 00:13:43.010
Can I really say that?

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:55.520
I'm home Bonny.

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:42.010
I wouldn't give a dog I liked
to that Blewett woman!

00:14:42.010 --> 00:14:43.100
What did you said?

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:46.060
No, nothing...

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:52.300
I don't fancy her style myself, but it's
that or keeping her ourselves, Matthew.

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:54.380
Well now...

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:58.130
And since you seem to want her, I
suppose I'm willing, or have to be.

00:14:58.130 --> 00:15:03.040
I've been thinking over the idea
until I've got kind of used to it.

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:11.200
I've never brought up a child,  and I
dare say I'll make a terrible mess of it.

00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:13.500
But I'll do my best.

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:19.570
Well now, I reckoned you'd come
to see it in that light, Marilla.

00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:23.900
She's such an interesting little thing.

00:15:23.900 --> 00:15:27.360
It'd be more to the point if you could
say she was a useful little thing.

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:31.990
W-well now... you are right...

00:15:31.990 --> 00:15:35.490
But I'll make it my business
to see she's trained to be that.

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:37.870
I'll teach her how to behave like a girl!

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:43.080
And mind, Matthew, you're not
to go interfering with my methods.

00:15:43.540 --> 00:15:47.620
There, there, Marilla,
you can have your own way.

00:15:49.210 --> 00:15:52.610
Only be as good and kind to her
as you can without spoiling her.

00:15:54.070 --> 00:15:58.190
You've always been afraid of females,
I'm surprised to hear this coming from you.

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:02.860
I won't tell her tonight that she can stay.

00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:06.590
She'd be so excited that
she wouldn't sleep a wink.

00:16:08.740 --> 00:16:11.000
I wonder how this experiment will turn out?

00:16:13.600 --> 00:16:16.980
I don't think it's a problem telling her tonight.

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:04.080
Anne, Anne, dinnertime.

00:17:04.080 --> 00:17:05.160
Yes!

00:17:07.130 --> 00:17:08.840
Anne, hurry down.

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:23.300
Have I fallen asleep?

00:18:06.910 --> 00:18:10.070
Now, Anne, last night
you threw your clothes

00:18:10.070 --> 00:18:12.070
all about the floor when you took them off.

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:16.500
When you take off any article of clothing
fold it neatly and place it on the chair.

00:18:16.500 --> 00:18:18.300
I haven't any use for
little girls who aren't neat.

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:23.140
I was so harrowed up in my mind last night
that I didn't think about my clothes at all.

00:18:23.670 --> 00:18:25.760
I'll fold them nicely tonight.

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:28.900
They always made us
do that at the asylum.

00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:31.530
Half the time, though, I'd forget.

00:18:31.540 --> 00:18:34.650
But I'll be very tidy tonight.

00:19:07.220 --> 00:19:09.760
Say your prayers now and get into bed.

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:12.080
I'll come and get the candle later.

00:19:12.730 --> 00:19:14.810
I never say any prayers.

00:19:16.670 --> 00:19:20.400
Why, Anne, what do you mean?
Were you never taught to say your prayers?

00:19:21.690 --> 00:19:23.130
Don't you know who God is?

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:26.600
God is a spirit, infinite, eternal
and unchangeable, in His being,

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:29.610
wisdom, power, holiness,
justice, goodness, and truth.

00:19:31.640 --> 00:19:34.330
So you do know something then,
thank goodness!

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:36.500
Where did you learn that?

00:19:36.830 --> 00:19:41.230
At the asylum Sunday-school.
They made us learn the whole catechism.

00:19:42.110 --> 00:19:46.220
I liked it pretty well. There's something
splendid about some of the words.

00:19:47.810 --> 00:19:51.640
Infinite, eternal and unchangeable.
Isn't that grand?

00:19:52.260 --> 00:19:56.000
It has such a roll to it.
just like a big organ playing.

00:19:56.680 --> 00:19:58.680
It sounds like poetry.

00:19:58.900 --> 00:20:02.970
We're not talking about poetry.
We are talking about saying your prayers.

00:20:03.700 --> 00:20:07.700
Don't you know it's a terrible wicked thing
not to say your prayers every night?

00:20:07.700 --> 00:20:09.810
I'm afraid you are a very bad little girl.

00:20:10.130 --> 00:20:13.780
You'd find it easier to be bad
than good if you had red hair.

00:20:15.510 --> 00:20:19.360
People who haven't red hair
don't know what trouble is.

00:20:19.970 --> 00:20:25.030
Mrs. Thomas told me that God
made my hair red on purpose,

00:20:25.030 --> 00:20:28.480
and I've never cared about him since.

00:20:29.300 --> 00:20:33.060
And anyhow I'd always be too tired at
night to bother saying prayers.

00:20:33.610 --> 00:20:37.110
People who have to look after twins
can't be expected to say their prayers.

00:20:37.970 --> 00:20:41.380
You must say your prayers
while you are under my roof.

00:20:43.310 --> 00:20:45.210
But how do I do it?

00:20:47.510 --> 00:20:53.090
I believe that it will be quite interesting,
now that I come to think of it.

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:55.830
You must kneel down.

00:20:59.990 --> 00:21:01.980
Why must people kneel down to pray?

00:21:03.510 --> 00:21:05.480
If I really wanted to pray,

00:21:05.750 --> 00:21:09.590
I'd go out into a great big field
or into the deep, deep, woods,

00:21:09.590 --> 00:21:10.690
and I'd look up.

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:18.560
I'd look up, up, into that lovely blue sky.

00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:24.720
And then I'd just feel a prayer.

00:21:28.580 --> 00:21:31.090
I'm ready. What am I to say?

00:21:34.100 --> 00:21:36.830
Now I lay me down to sleep...

00:21:42.790 --> 00:21:45.660
You're old enough to pray for yourself.

00:21:47.030 --> 00:21:49.250
Just thank God for your blessings,

00:21:49.250 --> 00:21:52.910
and ask Him humbly for the things you want.

00:21:54.090 --> 00:21:56.080
Well, I'll do my best.

00:21:59.470 --> 00:22:01.610
Gracious heavenly Father...

00:22:02.330 --> 00:22:04.950
that's the way the ministers say it in church.

00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:08.290
Gracious heavenly Father...

00:22:08.860 --> 00:22:12.430
I thank Thee for the White Way of Delight
and the Lake of Shining Waters

00:22:12.430 --> 00:22:16.520
and Bonny and the Snow Queen.

00:22:16.520 --> 00:22:18.260
I'm really grateful for them.

00:22:19.040 --> 00:22:23.230
The things I want, are so many that it would
take a great deal of time to name them all,

00:22:23.230 --> 00:22:25.960
so I will only mention the
two most important.

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:30.220
Please let me stay at Green Gables.

00:22:30.770 --> 00:22:35.340
And please let me be
good-looking when I grow up.

00:22:35.340 --> 00:22:38.990
Yours respectfully, Anne Shirley.

00:22:42.220 --> 00:22:43.350
Did I do all right?

00:22:44.760 --> 00:22:49.960
I could have made it much more flowery
if I'd had a little more time to think it over.

00:22:51.890 --> 00:22:53.880
Alright, go to bed now.

00:22:54.030 --> 00:22:55.640
Yes. Goodnight.

00:23:07.340 --> 00:23:08.780
I've just thought of it now.

00:23:08.780 --> 00:23:13.100
I should have said, "Amen" in place of
"yours respectfully",

00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:14.880
the way the ministers do.

00:23:15.230 --> 00:23:16.980
Do you suppose it will make any difference?

00:23:17.830 --> 00:23:21.040
I... I don't suppose it will.

00:23:21.040 --> 00:23:23.800
I'm relieved. Goodnight.

00:23:35.080 --> 00:23:36.110
Matthew Cuthbert,

00:23:37.130 --> 00:23:40.460
will you believe that she never said
a prayer in her life till tonight?

00:23:42.130 --> 00:23:45.000
She shall go to Sunday-school
just as soon as I can get

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:47.000
some suitable clothes made for her.

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:49.400
I foresee that I shall have
my hands full.

00:23:50.520 --> 00:23:57.510
Well, well, we can't get through this
world without our share of trouble.

00:23:57.510 --> 00:24:02.440
I've had a pretty easy life so far,
but my time has come at last.

00:24:02.440 --> 00:24:06.820
Well now, you'll do alright.

00:24:17.320 --> 00:24:18.600
On the next day...

00:24:18.600 --> 00:24:24.290
Anne was exhilarated upon
learning that she can stay.

00:24:24.690 --> 00:24:29.900
Next of Anne of Green Gables:
Anne of Green Gables.

00:24:29.900 --> 00:24:30.460
Please watch!
